WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.465 --> 00:00:02.678
- How are you doing Mr. Deniro,
my name's Bradley Cooper.

00:00:02.678 --> 00:00:04.665
My question is regarding awakenings.

00:00:04.665 --> 00:00:07.600
And I remember thinking if doesn't work

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.889
maybe I'm not right for
this business, I thought.

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:11.317
You know people talk about
how you need a vacation

00:00:11.317 --> 00:00:13.743
or something, a vacation from what?

00:00:13.743 --> 00:00:16.495
I think that if you love what you do,

00:00:16.495 --> 00:00:19.750
isn't it hard to be an
asshole, I don't know?

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.027
- He's one of the highest
paid actors in the world

00:00:22.027 --> 00:00:24.946
and he's been nominated
for four Academy Awards.

00:00:24.946 --> 00:00:28.725
His breakthrough came in 2009
with his film The Hangover.

00:00:28.725 --> 00:00:32.801
Time magazine named him in
2015, one of the 100 most

00:00:32.801 --> 00:00:35.390
influential people in the world.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.004
He's Bradley Cooper and here's my take

00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:38.981
on his top 10 rules of success.

00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:40.535
Rule number four is my personal favorite

00:00:40.535 --> 00:00:43.334
and curious to figure out
which ones you like the best.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:45.351
Also as Bradley's talking
if he says something

00:00:45.351 --> 00:00:46.713
that really really resonates with you,

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.438
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:48.438 --> 00:00:49.579
and put quotes around it

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:53.496
so other people can be
inspired as well, enjoy.

00:00:55.411 --> 00:00:58.578
(inspirational music)

00:01:05.636 --> 00:01:07.480
- Want to go for a ride in my Porsche?

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.850
- [Announcer] From the one
time role on Sex and the City.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.145
- You know what I got to go home.

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:13.444
- No way, I'm not letting you out.

00:01:13.444 --> 00:01:16.624
My shirt doesn't have
enough ice cream on it.

00:01:16.624 --> 00:01:18.790
- [Announcer] To his
recurring role on Alias,

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.482
bit parts in movies, but it
all changed six years ago.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:25.443
- To a night, the four
of us will never forget.

00:01:25.443 --> 00:01:26.790
- [Announcer] With The Hangover.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.391
- How does one go from The Hangover movies

00:01:28.391 --> 00:01:32.936
to this very serious, in part
dark characters in some way.

00:01:32.936 --> 00:01:36.478
- Well I don't see any difference at all.

00:01:36.478 --> 00:01:37.395
- Huh?

00:01:37.395 --> 00:01:39.584
- I don't, I mean that Phil Winnick to me

00:01:39.584 --> 00:01:41.673
was a real character that wasn't me.

00:01:41.673 --> 00:01:43.089
Tracy, it's Phil.

00:01:43.089 --> 00:01:44.647
- [Tracy] Phil where
the hell are you guys?

00:01:44.647 --> 00:01:47.484
- And Todd Phillips is an auteur director

00:01:47.484 --> 00:01:50.208
and Zac Galifianakis, Ed Helms are as good

00:01:50.208 --> 00:01:52.394
as Christian Bale and Jeremy Renner.

00:01:52.394 --> 00:01:55.153
I mean, those guys are
serious gangster actors,

00:01:55.153 --> 00:01:57.522
I mean they are no joke and
you got to bring it hard

00:01:57.522 --> 00:01:58.794
every time you're with them.

00:01:58.794 --> 00:02:00.681
So I actually see it as the same thing.

00:02:00.681 --> 00:02:02.741
How you doing Mr. Deniro,
my name's Bradly Cooper.

00:02:02.741 --> 00:02:05.295
My question is regarding awakenings.

00:02:05.295 --> 00:02:08.511
I wanted desperately to ask
a question, I was terrified.

00:02:08.511 --> 00:02:10.682
And I wanted to ask this
specific random question

00:02:10.682 --> 00:02:14.907
about awakenings and
right before I came to my,

00:02:14.907 --> 00:02:16.690
because you have to say
before that you want to

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.465
and they pass you the mic
so I thought this is going

00:02:19.465 --> 00:02:22.020
to be a stupid question, he's going to say

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.504
why would you ask that stupid question.

00:02:24.504 --> 00:02:27.139
And so I thought I'll just
ask some bull question

00:02:27.139 --> 00:02:29.392
about The Mission and riding a horse.

00:02:29.392 --> 00:02:32.431
And then somebody asked a
question about The Mission

00:02:32.431 --> 00:02:35.041
right before my question
and I thought oh no,

00:02:35.041 --> 00:02:36.210
next thing you know it's my turn,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.308
I'm standing up and I'm looking
at my idol 30 feet away.

00:02:40.308 --> 00:02:41.951
And then I just asked
the question I wanted to

00:02:41.951 --> 00:02:46.122
about awakenings and
he said good question.

00:02:46.122 --> 00:02:50.289
And it blew me away, it
was just a little reminder,

00:02:51.238 --> 00:02:53.178
you know what just go with your gut.

00:02:53.178 --> 00:02:56.270
There's a reason why
you think the way you do

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:58.280
and want to do the things you do

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.488
and you just can't let
anybody tell you don't do it.

00:03:01.488 --> 00:03:04.125
But especially you,
don't allow you to tell

00:03:04.125 --> 00:03:05.448
you not to do it.

00:03:05.448 --> 00:03:09.615
The loudest voice that's a
contrarian is usually me.

00:03:12.106 --> 00:03:14.708
All these little moments of confirmation

00:03:14.708 --> 00:03:17.222
that that voice should just shut up.

00:03:17.222 --> 00:03:19.387
Allows you to just do
what you're supposed to do

00:03:19.387 --> 00:03:20.740
while you're here.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.615
- [Interviewer] Between
those things you also

00:03:22.615 --> 00:03:24.902
went to Broadway, with Julia
Roberts, with Paul Rudd,

00:03:24.902 --> 00:03:27.079
Three Days of Rain, that must
have been pretty special.

00:03:27.079 --> 00:03:31.522
- Yeah it was a do or die
moment for me actually.

00:03:31.522 --> 00:03:34.939
Kitchen Confidential had gotten canceled,

00:03:36.512 --> 00:03:40.474
and I remember thinking
if this doesn't work,

00:03:40.474 --> 00:03:42.865
maybe I'm not right for
this business, I thought.

00:03:42.865 --> 00:03:44.706
The moments in life meaning, you know what

00:03:44.706 --> 00:03:47.103
it kind of does matter if this fails.

00:03:47.103 --> 00:03:49.193
Or you fail in it.

00:03:49.193 --> 00:03:52.502
And it was hard, it was the hardest thing

00:03:52.502 --> 00:03:55.002
I've ever been through by far.

00:03:56.089 --> 00:03:58.799
I remember thinking if
I can get through this,

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:01.329
it'll may be as hard but it's impossible

00:04:01.329 --> 00:04:02.814
for it to be harder and it's true,

00:04:02.814 --> 00:04:05.956
I've had a lot of hard
times since jobs you know,

00:04:05.956 --> 00:04:07.960
where you're like wow,
the pressure's so intense

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.969
but it always, I have such a foundation,

00:04:09.969 --> 00:04:11.014
from Three Days of Rain.

00:04:11.014 --> 00:04:12.697
- [Interviewer] I was going
to say eight shows a week--

00:04:12.697 --> 00:04:15.129
- But not even that, actually
it was the rehearsal process.

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:18.479
Not the, once we got on stage
it flew and that thank God,

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:20.058
and I thought oh this feels right.

00:04:20.058 --> 00:04:24.225
And I was hard on myself but
it was just, man it was tough.

00:04:25.857 --> 00:04:28.115
- [Announcer] The Elephant
Man has been a huge success.

00:04:28.115 --> 00:04:30.977
Smashing attendance records
and winning Cooper excellent

00:04:30.977 --> 00:04:33.566
reviews for Broadway's toughest critics

00:04:33.566 --> 00:04:36.010
for a role that's been a
part of his consciousness

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.523
for decades.

00:04:37.523 --> 00:04:40.014
At age 12 he decided he
wanted to be an actor

00:04:40.014 --> 00:04:42.796
after watching the movie version.

00:04:42.796 --> 00:04:44.712
It tells the story of John Merrick,

00:04:44.712 --> 00:04:46.643
a horribly deformed British man

00:04:46.643 --> 00:04:50.000
with a saintly soul who's
rescued from a 19th century

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.197
freak show and given sanctuary
at a London hospital.

00:04:54.197 --> 00:04:58.068
28 years later, Cooper has
produced, cast, and arranged

00:04:58.068 --> 00:05:00.971
the financing for the
current Broadway production.

00:05:00.971 --> 00:05:01.932
- [Interviewer] You play John Merrick.

00:05:01.932 --> 00:05:02.765
- [Bradley] Yeah.

00:05:02.765 --> 00:05:07.392
- Without any makeup,
without any prosthetics.

00:05:07.392 --> 00:05:08.697
Just you Bradley Cooper.

00:05:08.697 --> 00:05:09.530
- Yeah.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.751
- And you have to convince
the audience that you're him.

00:05:11.751 --> 00:05:12.875
- Yeah.

00:05:12.875 --> 00:05:14.027
- How do you do that?

00:05:14.027 --> 00:05:16.002
- By believing I'm him.

00:05:16.002 --> 00:05:17.065
That's how I do it.

00:05:17.065 --> 00:05:20.165
If I'm acting like I'm
him or don't quite make

00:05:20.165 --> 00:05:22.422
that leap of faith,
there's absolutely no way

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:23.997
you're going to believe it.

00:05:23.997 --> 00:05:25.320
I had to do a tremendous amount of work

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.165
to get to a place where
I do believe I'm him.

00:05:28.165 --> 00:05:30.307
- [Announcer] Part of it
was 20 years of research

00:05:30.307 --> 00:05:33.848
and part of it a performance
unlike he's ever given.

00:05:33.848 --> 00:05:35.787
The transformation from Cooper to Merrick

00:05:35.787 --> 00:05:39.467
is shared by the audience
in one of early scenes.

00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:41.708
- [Bradley] As the doctor is speaking

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:43.000
to the pathological society

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.906
which is actually is the
audience of the play,

00:05:44.906 --> 00:05:48.816
and you see an actual
photograph of Joseph Merrick.

00:05:48.816 --> 00:05:51.344
He is then describing
all of his afflictions.

00:05:51.344 --> 00:05:53.852
- [Man] From the upper
jaw, the projected another

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:55.132
mass of bone.

00:05:55.132 --> 00:05:58.467
- And then I then interpret
each one in a physical manner

00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:02.467
- [Man] The right hand
was large and clumsy, fin

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:05.294
or paddle, rather than a hand.

00:06:05.294 --> 00:06:08.464
- [Bradley] Almost mimicry,
almost like a mime.

00:06:08.464 --> 00:06:10.548
- To farther burden to his trouble,

00:06:10.548 --> 00:06:14.785
the wretched man will
avoid developed hip disease

00:06:14.785 --> 00:06:17.101
which left him permanently lame

00:06:17.101 --> 00:06:19.905
so that he would only walk with a stick.

00:06:19.905 --> 00:06:23.609
- And once he's finished
with that presentation.

00:06:23.609 --> 00:06:26.143
I'm fully physically transformed

00:06:26.143 --> 00:06:28.634
but the soul hasn't been injected yet.

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:31.637
He brings the cane over, steps away,

00:06:31.637 --> 00:06:34.209
and then says please.

00:06:34.209 --> 00:06:35.042
- [Man] Please.

00:06:35.042 --> 00:06:38.516
(Bradley groans)

00:06:38.516 --> 00:06:42.545
- And that's the first time
you hear Merrick alive.

00:06:42.545 --> 00:06:46.712
That is the moment that
the transformation occurs.

00:06:48.025 --> 00:06:49.934
And if I don't make that
leap at that moment,

00:06:49.934 --> 00:06:52.274
the rest of the play is not going to work.

00:06:52.274 --> 00:06:54.700
Absolutely, I try to find different things

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:58.625
for no other reason
than to further explore.

00:06:58.625 --> 00:07:00.403
I have no desire to repeat anything

00:07:00.403 --> 00:07:03.135
and I want to constantly learn.

00:07:03.135 --> 00:07:06.098
Now I'm in a situation where
I'm very lucky right now

00:07:06.098 --> 00:07:08.626
and it won't last forever,
it may not last another year,

00:07:08.626 --> 00:07:13.091
but to really be at a place
where it's not just about,

00:07:13.091 --> 00:07:15.472
'cause for a while it's,
I just got to get a job

00:07:15.472 --> 00:07:19.639
and now for as long as it
lasts, I'm able to read a book

00:07:20.727 --> 00:07:23.321
like American Sniper and
then try to produce it

00:07:23.321 --> 00:07:26.414
and could go to a director
like Clint Eastwood,

00:07:26.414 --> 00:07:28.440
I mean these are things
that if you told me that

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.607
as a young actor in grad
school, you got to be joking.

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:36.321
But that said, I remember being on Alias

00:07:36.321 --> 00:07:40.066
and I was playing this
character who was only good

00:07:40.066 --> 00:07:43.012
it felt like, I remember
feeling like, I got to play

00:07:43.012 --> 00:07:45.482
something else and
really being so grateful

00:07:45.482 --> 00:07:47.101
that I got the role in Wedding Crashers

00:07:47.101 --> 00:07:50.312
where he was so diametrically
opposed to that character.

00:07:50.312 --> 00:07:53.969
And I thought early on,
really keep trying to go here

00:07:53.969 --> 00:07:57.440
and here and I used to, I
always remember Willem Dafoe

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.280
came to our school and
talked about how he only

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.771
takes roles that scare him.

00:08:00.771 --> 00:08:04.175
And I remember thinking, or
roles that scare him, roles that

00:08:04.175 --> 00:08:06.693
he thinks he can't do, and I
thought I think I would only

00:08:06.693 --> 00:08:08.539
want to do things I thought I could do.

00:08:08.539 --> 00:08:10.757
And it was interesting
I used to think that

00:08:10.757 --> 00:08:13.259
and now it's more like
things that scare me.

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:15.775
Chris Kyle, if you told me
I could have played him,

00:08:15.775 --> 00:08:17.519
I didn't even when we were developing,

00:08:17.519 --> 00:08:19.459
I thought well I'll just
develop the material,

00:08:19.459 --> 00:08:21.101
tell the studio that I'll play him

00:08:21.101 --> 00:08:22.835
but really I won't play him.

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:24.257
I'll get another actor who I thought

00:08:24.257 --> 00:08:27.433
would've been perfect for it, honestly.

00:08:27.433 --> 00:08:30.819
And Phil with The Hangover, it was funny

00:08:30.819 --> 00:08:32.798
because you mentioned that
because we were saying

00:08:32.798 --> 00:08:35.939
that I saw it last night
and I was telling my friend.

00:08:35.939 --> 00:08:38.345
Looking at that guy then,
I was so the opposite

00:08:38.345 --> 00:08:40.857
of that guy because I was
going to give up the business,

00:08:40.857 --> 00:08:44.002
I was doing a play at
Williamstown and I thought,

00:08:44.002 --> 00:08:46.576
I just can't it's not going to
be, it's not going to happen

00:08:46.576 --> 00:08:48.550
because I knew I only really wanted to be

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.559
in the middle of story-telling.

00:08:50.559 --> 00:08:52.767
I love making a movie, I
don't love just acting,

00:08:52.767 --> 00:08:54.349
but I love the whole thing.

00:08:54.349 --> 00:08:56.358
And then Todd Phillips called me,

00:08:56.358 --> 00:08:58.943
or emailed me and said
let's do this bitches

00:08:58.943 --> 00:09:01.276
and then we were filming it.

00:09:02.188 --> 00:09:05.677
And Phil's like this very confident guy

00:09:05.677 --> 00:09:07.900
and I was at a point in
my life I wouldn't say,

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:11.666
and I'm with these massive
juggernaut comedians

00:09:11.666 --> 00:09:14.100
who are crushing and for Phil to come off

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.353
the way he did, I don't
know how that happened

00:09:18.353 --> 00:09:19.766
because I wasn't in that place.

00:09:19.766 --> 00:09:22.231
So I always feel like
that I try to do things

00:09:22.231 --> 00:09:24.241
that don't come easy.

00:09:24.241 --> 00:09:26.343
But that only happened later.

00:09:26.343 --> 00:09:29.253
People talk about oh you
need a vacation or something.

00:09:29.253 --> 00:09:30.505
A vacation from what?

00:09:30.505 --> 00:09:33.686
I love my life, I feel,
I'm healthy thank God

00:09:33.686 --> 00:09:36.554
and you know, I have
these incredible directors

00:09:36.554 --> 00:09:38.916
that are interested in working with me

00:09:38.916 --> 00:09:41.244
and I'm going to jump at that as long

00:09:41.244 --> 00:09:42.532
as there's enough time to prep

00:09:42.532 --> 00:09:46.311
and I feel like I can add
to it, yeah I mean for sure.

00:09:46.311 --> 00:09:47.223
- [Interviewer] Has it changed in terms,

00:09:47.223 --> 00:09:48.743
I guess there's a point
where you don't have

00:09:48.743 --> 00:09:50.660
to chase quite as hard.

00:09:51.821 --> 00:09:53.214
- You know it depends on the project.

00:09:53.214 --> 00:09:54.047
I still chase.

00:09:54.047 --> 00:09:56.517
I wrote an email to a
director about six months ago

00:09:56.517 --> 00:09:59.436
about a project that I
felt so connected to,

00:09:59.436 --> 00:10:01.091
never got a response.

00:10:01.091 --> 00:10:02.363
- [Interviewer] That's surprising.

00:10:02.363 --> 00:10:04.446
- But you never you know.

00:10:05.304 --> 00:10:06.217
- [Interviewer] You just
think professional courtesy

00:10:06.217 --> 00:10:07.145
would be enough--

00:10:07.145 --> 00:10:09.312
- Oh not in this business.

00:10:10.269 --> 00:10:13.339
- [Interviewer] Do you
have a career idea in,

00:10:13.339 --> 00:10:14.897
do you have a mindset?

00:10:14.897 --> 00:10:17.533
- Uh, no, I mean yeah I
do, mindset is so simple.

00:10:17.533 --> 00:10:19.054
I just want to get better.

00:10:19.054 --> 00:10:20.184
I want to get better and I want to work

00:10:20.184 --> 00:10:23.105
with great people, it's really simple.

00:10:23.105 --> 00:10:24.888
- [Interviewer] Do you want
to pushed in new directions

00:10:24.888 --> 00:10:25.845
all the time or you--

00:10:25.845 --> 00:10:29.263
- I don't want to be pushed,
I think I would push myself.

00:10:29.263 --> 00:10:33.346
I have a pretty strong
engine of wanting to grow.

00:10:36.231 --> 00:10:37.488
Although David is a good example,

00:10:37.488 --> 00:10:40.081
I didn't think I could do
that role and he was example

00:10:40.081 --> 00:10:43.436
of a director saying just walk with me.

00:10:43.436 --> 00:10:45.334
And we're going to do
another movie together

00:10:45.334 --> 00:10:47.025
in the spring where he's,
it's the same thing,

00:10:47.025 --> 00:10:49.404
it's a difficult character.

00:10:49.404 --> 00:10:51.797
- [Woman] I had the opportunity to play

00:10:51.797 --> 00:10:55.880
one of your background
nurses on American Sniper.

00:10:56.748 --> 00:10:58.861
You were in Inglewood in the hospital

00:10:58.861 --> 00:11:01.478
and you spoke to us, you were so nice,

00:11:01.478 --> 00:11:04.810
and I remember meeting
you were, you greeted us,

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:08.190
and I was like oh my God,
he's such an A-list actor,

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.970
a principle, and he's so
nice to us background.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.470
So my question is how do you stay so nice?

00:11:18.434 --> 00:11:21.965
- Well you know I think
the question perplexes me

00:11:21.965 --> 00:11:24.488
at the root and maybe that's why

00:11:24.488 --> 00:11:26.655
because why wouldn't I be?

00:11:28.509 --> 00:11:31.090
Yeah but I don't know you're a human being

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:33.035
and we're all on set together
trying to tell a story,

00:11:33.035 --> 00:11:35.182
to me it's always very clear
and that's the real truth,

00:11:35.182 --> 00:11:37.718
everything else is a sort
of artificial, branding,

00:11:37.718 --> 00:11:41.051
or ladder, I don't know what that means,

